server: a native arm64 wineserver serves translated x86 guests.

Origin: Dormison (github.com/kageroumado/dormison), commit 3b55f4eac, Copyright (c) kageroumado,
LGPL-2.1-or-later like Wine. Only the server/ part of that commit is here; its dock-shim and
package-engine.sh parts are Dormison's packaging. Checked to apply unchanged to WineForge 0.6.0.0-0.6.0.7
(engine-fixes/rehearse.sh, 2026-10-18); not built or run on WineForge yet.

Why: the engine's wineserver runs no guest code, so it need not be translated. Built for arm64 it is a
process the Rosetta tax does not touch; Dormison measured it at parity with the translated one (p50 0.98x,
p99 1.01x, server CPU 0.96x), so the case is one translated process fewer, not frame rate. Our own
syncbench agrees: the wineserver sync rates of Dormison r6 (arm64) and WineForge 0.6.0.7 (x86_64) are
within 1-2 % of each other.

What it does: upstream asks "is the server itself running under Rosetta" (sysctl proc_translated), which
is false for an arm64 server whose clients are all translated. The server now asks about the client
process (kinfo_proc.kp_proc.p_flag & P_TRANSLATED) in get/set_thread_context and write_process_memory.
registry.c reports the x86 machines when the server is built with -DDORMISON_X86_64_GUEST.

How to build it: a second, arm64 configure tree for server/ only, beside the x86_64 one:
  CC='clang -arch arm64 -std=gnu23' CFLAGS='-g -O2 -DDORMISON_X86_64_GUEST'   (Dormison's
  build-macos/build-native-server.sh; it also builds a universal libinotify). The result replaces
  bin/wineserver. The engine's own x86_64 wineserver stays as the fallback.
Related Dormison commits not taken here (they fail on every WineForge release and need a look first):
90bd24fa0 (ask a process once whether it runs under Rosetta: a cache), 5562e98ef (signal a dead thread's
handle once its Mach thread is gone: msync+ related).

